<h1 class="reading-title">Sandhill Crane Was Never Supposed to Be Here</h1>
<div class="reading-body">
<h2>The Arrival</h2>
<p>The sandhill crane lands in my father's hospital parking lot at 6:47 AM, December 3rd&mdash;two thousand miles from where it should be, three months past migration. I know because I'm watching from the oncology wing's seventh floor, where Lou has been dying for eleven days, transforming the word <em>wisdom</em> into something I no longer recognize.</p>
<p>My credentials for telling this: I've spent twenty-three years translating my father's Hmong proverbs into psychiatric terminology for doctors who think metaphor is a symptom. I hold a PhD in ornithology that I earned trying to understand why he called me <em>noog</em>&mdash;bird&mdash;when he meant daughter. I am the only person who knows that Lou Vang once painted sandhill cranes on artillery shells in Laos, 1969, believing beauty could defuse bombs.</p>
<p>The crane stands motionless between two ambulances, its red crown catching first light. Rare, very rare this time of year. The phrase runs through my mind in Lou's voice, though he hasn't spoken in three days.</p>
<h2>The Investigation Thread</h2>
<p><em>University of Minnesota Raptor Center, two weeks earlier. Dr. Sarah Chen pulls up migration maps.</em></p>
<p>"Sandhill cranes winter in Texas, New Mexico, Arizona," she says, tracing routes with her finger. "Never Minnesota in December. If you saw one&mdash;"</p>
<p>"It would be lost," I finish.</p>
<p>"Or injured. Or something's shifted in its internal compass."</p>
<p>She doesn't know about Lou's theory: that certain birds carry messages between worlds, appearing where they're needed rather than where they belong. As a child, I dismissed this as refugee superstition. Now, watching my father's body fail while his mind travels elsewhere, I'm documenting every bird that appears at his window.</p>
<p>November 22: Three crows<br>November 28: Cardinal, female<br>December 1: Hawk, species unknown<br>December 3: Sandhill crane, impossible</p>
<p>Dr. Chen shows me data from the International Crane Foundation. Climate change has altered some migration patterns, but not like this. "Unless," she adds, "someone's been feeding them. Creating false anchor points."</p>
<p>I think of Lou scattering rice in our backyard for thirty years, calling birds in Hmong, teaching them words they couldn't possibly understand.</p>
</div>
